A||||3||ponya||Brake bleed problem||||5||
Z||000000||ponya||11-02-2000||05:58 AM||||My son did the rear brake upgrade on my 86SE last weekend. Everything in front and back is now new. Problem is that the brakes are still mushy. I know that this indicates that there is probably air in the system. He also replaced the master cylinder and bench bled it, but he says that he keeps getting air. Could it be a bad master cylinder or is there something that we are overlooking. Any suggestions apprecitated.  Thanks in advance,<P>Bob||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||11-02-2000||08:47 AM||||Are all the hoses in good shape? Get them all tight with new copper washers? Any seeping in the lines anywhere? 'NEW' parts or used? Air all over or single wheel(s)||||reg||1
Z||000002||lawrence||11-02-2000||12:38 PM||||Bench bleeding a master cylinder is tricky.  You have to lightly rap the body of the cylinder with a hammer to make sure all the air gets out of the trapped spaces.<P>I always mount the cylinder in a vise, then tilt it forward and back to make sure all the air is out.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||11-02-2000||01:25 PM||||Autozone or most any parts store have brake line bleeder tool/guns.  They don't cost much and you don't have to have a person sit and pump the brakes.  Might save you some time.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||1
